   "answer_key": {
      "form": "statement",
      "label": "The integral converges to \\( 7 \\sqrt{3} \\)."
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The integral is improper because the integrand is unbounded as x approaches 1 from the right, so write it as \\( \\lim_{R\\to 1^{+}} \\int_{R}^{2} \\frac{7 x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}}\\,dx \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99053",
         "rule_named": "improper integral as a limit"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Substitute \\( u = x^{2} - 1,\\ du = 2 x\\,dx \\). The limits become \\( u = R^{2} - 1 \\text{ to } u = 3 \\), and an antiderivative in u is \\( 7 \\sqrt{u} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99003",
         "rule_named": "substitution with changed limits"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluate the limit: \\( \\lim_{R\\to 1^{+}} \\left( 7 \\sqrt{3} - 7\\sqrt{R^{2} - 1} \\right) = 7 \\sqrt{3} \\). The limit is finite, so the integral converges to \\( 7 \\sqrt{3} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99005",
         "rule_named": "limit of the definite integrals"
      }
   ],
